// std.metrics cardinality: a host-owned instrument aggregates one series per attribute set, so an
// attribute whose value differs per measurement — a request id, a user id — would otherwise grow a
// map that lives as long as the process. Each instrument therefore aggregates at most a fixed number
// of attribute sets separately (2000, configurable with `OTEL_METRIC_CARDINALITY_LIMIT`); every set
// past that folds into the single `otel.metric.overflow=true` series rather than being dropped, so
// the counter's total stays exact while its breakdown stops. The cap is per instrument, so a second
// counter used correctly is unaffected by the first one's cardinality.
// A metric is a write-only side effect (aggregated host-side, never program output), so this
// program's OWN output is what the differential pins; that the series stay bounded and the total
// survives is asserted on both backends in noeta-conformance's `metrics` sink-parity oracle.
// expect: stdout "start"
// expect: stdout "2500"
// expect: stdout "2100"
// expect: stdout "done"
// expect: exit 0
use std.metrics

echo "start"

// A high-cardinality attribute: a distinct value on every single measurement.
reqs = metrics.counter("http.requests")
mut i = 0;
while i < 2500 {
    reqs.add_with(1, {"request.id": i});
    i += 1;
}
echo i

// A low-cardinality attribute on its own instrument — a route template, which is what an attribute
// value should look like. Its own budget is untouched by the counter above.
routes = metrics.counter("http.routes")
routes.add_with(1, {"route": "/orders"})
routes.add_with(1, {"route": "/users"})
routes.add_with(1, {"route": "/orders"})

// The cap is on the instrument, not on counters: this histogram passes the same limit, and its
// overflow series keeps the folded measurements' count, sum and bucket distribution.
lat = metrics.histogram("http.latency")
mut k = 0;
while k < 2100 {
    lat.record_with(1.5, {"request.id": k});
    k += 1;
}
echo k

// A gauge is last-value, so it has no total to keep — which is why its attribute values want to be
// the most conservative of the four. This one is low-cardinality and never folds.
depth = metrics.gauge("queue.depth")
depth.record_with(3, {"queue": "orders"})

echo "done"
